        "suggestion": 0
      },
      "summary": "The tests map to the behavioral acceptance criteria and exercise intake routing, child-process parsing, time and memory limits, the decompression bomb, and the empty environment. I found no in-scope test defect.",
      "findingList": []
    },
    {
      "agentId": "security-reviewer",
      "reviewType": "security",
      "harness": "codex",
      "harnessVersion": "0.162.0",
      "model": "gpt-6-luna",
      "approved": false,
      "findings": {
        "critical": 0,
        "major": 1,
        "minor": 0,
        "suggestion": 0
      },
      "summary": "The Linux worker can change the permissions on its own working directory, so the configured read-only mode does not hold if parsing is exploited.",
      "findingList": [
        {
          "severity": "major",
          "file": "backend/AirlineDemo.Api/Evidence/IsolatedPdfParser.cs",
          "line": 148,
          "message": "The Linux working-directory mode can be changed by the worker because it runs as the directory owner. Run the worker under an identity that cannot change the directory permissions or use a restriction it cannot bypass.",
          "confidence": 0.84,
          "category": "security A02:2025",
          "evidence": {
            "codePathTraced": "On Linux, RestrictWorkingDirectory sets the directory to mode 0555, but the worker normally runs with the API process identity, which owns that directory. The owner can change its permissions.",
            "failureScenario": "If a crafted PDF exploits the parser on a non-root Linux deployment, code in the worker can chmod its working directory to add write permission, then create files there. The directory is therefore not effectively read-only to the worker."
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
